.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-HBA 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-hba
.Nd report the storage controllers, their firmware and the drives behind them, without vendor tools
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm status | drives | enclosures | json
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Before a pool is trusted to a machine, its storage controllers' firmware
matters, and the programs that normally report it for SAS and RAID cards
are proprietary downloads that a darksite mirror may not carry.
.Nm
reports everything the kernel itself knows, from
.Pa /sys ,
.Xr lspci 8
and
.Xr smartctl 8 ,
and says what only a vendor tool could add.
It changes nothing: it never writes to a controller, and it does not
flash firmware.
.Pp
Controllers are found by PCI class \(em SCSI, RAID, SAS, SATA and NVMe \(em
never by vendor name.
A machine with no SAS or RAID card, which is every desktop board, is
normal, and said so.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm status
The default.
Each storage controller with its PCI address, class, driver and name; for
each SCSI host under it, the firmware and BIOS versions and board name
where the driver exposes them, and whether the card is in IT or RAID mode,
inferred from which driver took it; otherwise the number of drives behind
it.
Then the SES enclosures, and what cannot be known without a vendor tool.
.It Cm drives
Every physical drive: its most specific
.Pa /dev/disk/by-id
name, model, serial, firmware revision and SMART health, and a count.
Volumes, loop, RAM, device-mapper and optical devices are left out.
Serial, firmware and health need
.Xr smartctl 8 ,
and so need root.
.It Cm enclosures
For each SES enclosure, each slot, the drive in it and the slot's status:
which bay holds which disk.
.It Cm json
Controllers, drives and enclosures as one JSON object on standard output,
for an inventory or a label printer.
Every text field is escaped.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help , Cm help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev SYS_PCI , Ev SYS_HOST , Ev SYS_ENCL
Read these instead of
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/devices ,
.Pa /sys/class/scsi_host
and
.Pa /sys/class/enclosure ;
for testing against a copy.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
The report ran.
.It 1
A step failed.
.It 2
An unknown command.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30.
.Ss A desktop board
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-hba status
CONTROLLERS
  0000:01:00.0 NVMe  nvme           Samsung Electronics Co Ltd NVMe SSD Controller PM9A1/PM9A3
  0000:02:00.1 SATA  ahci           Advanced Micro Devices, Inc. [AMD] 500 Series Chipset SATA
      host0    0 drive(s); this driver exposes no firmware version in sysfs
\&...
      host5    0 drive(s); this driver exposes no firmware version in sysfs
  0000:38:00.0 NVMe  nvme           Sandisk Corp WD Black SN850X NVMe SSD (rev 01)

ENCLOSURES
  none (no SES backplane — expected on a desktop board)

WHAT THIS CANNOT TELL YOU
  No SAS/RAID HBA on this machine, so there is no vendor firmware to read.
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Drives, without their serial numbers
.Cm drives
prints serials, and by-id names that contain them; this page does not.
The same facts, from the JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-hba json | jq -r '.drives[] | [.kernel, .model, .firmware, .health] | @tsv'
nvme0n1	Samsung SSD 980 PRO 2TB	5B2QGXA7	PASSED
nvme1n1	WD_BLACK SN850X 4000GB	624361WD	PASSED
# kldload-hba json | jq -c '.controllers[]'
{"pci":"0000:01:00.0","class":"NVMe","driver":"nvme","fw":"","bios":"","board":"","hba":false}
{"pci":"0000:02:00.1","class":"SATA","driver":"ahci","fw":"","bios":"","board":"","hba":false}
{"pci":"0000:38:00.0","class":"NVMe","driver":"nvme","fw":"","bios":"","board":"","hba":false}
# kldload-hba json | jq -c '.enclosures'
[]
.Ed
.Pp
As a user, the columns that need
.Xr smartctl 8
are
.Ql -
and it says why:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-hba json 2>err | jq -r '.drives[] | [.kernel, .model, .firmware, .health] | @tsv'
nvme0n1	Samsung SSD 980 PRO 2TB	-	-
nvme1n1	WD_BLACK SN850X 4000GB	-	-
$ cat err
kldload-hba: not root: serial, firmware and health need smartctl as root
.Ed
.Ss Enclosures
The desktop has none:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-hba enclosures
No SES enclosures. On a desktop board this is normal; on a Storinator it
means the backplane is not presenting SES, which is worth chasing.
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
Against a made-up enclosure of two slots, one holding
.Pa sdz ,
built under a scratch directory
.Ar dir :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ SYS_ENCL=dir/encl kldload-hba enclosures
0:0:9:0
  slot 1    sdz        status=OK
  slot 2    -          status=not installed
.Ed
.Ss A mistake
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-hba frob
kldload-hba: unknown subcommand: frob
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jq 1 ,
.Xr lspci 8 ,
.Xr lsscsi 8 ,
.Xr sg_ses 8 ,
.Xr smartctl 8 ,
.Xr zpool 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-11 on a desktop it counted the ZFS volumes behind a hundred
virtual machines as drives, and died after two real ones on
.Xr smartctl 8 Ns 's
exit status, which is a bit mask.
On 2026-09-12 on a server with five identical drives, its fixed-width
column cut every by-id name where the serial begins, printing five paths
that did not exist and looked alike; and the JSON had no drives or
enclosures at all.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: every enclosure slot's status printed
empty, firmware and board text went into the JSON unescaped, and the
columns a user cannot read were blank without a word.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
NVMe drives are their own controllers and have no SCSI host, so
.Cm status
shows no drive count or firmware under them; the firmware is in
.Cm drives .
.Pp
IT or RAID mode is inferred from the driver, not read from the card.
